hy the reflection takes the dimensions of the space, not the square. For example, the top space, with 3 rows, reflects to rows 1-2, cols 2-3 with 3s. The bottom row, row 5, is filled with the value from the top-left coordinate.\n\n\n**Mapping the Test Input**\n\nI'm now applying my understanding to a test case. Based on a 2x2 square with values 1, 2, 3, and 4, I'm analyzing the reflections. The key focus is determining the new positions of colors in the target areas, and their respective dimensions. Dimensions of the space around the block matter. It seems my understanding of the square's color shifting is working well in this new scenario.\n\n\n**Constructing the Output**\n\nI'm now building the full output grid based on the transformation rules I've deduced. I've successfully placed the original 2x2 square in its designated position, and am populating the reflected quadrants. Row 0 and Row 1 are almost correct, with values from the initial quadrant being reflected as expected. I am confident in my construction approach, as it adheres to the point-reflection mechanism.\n\n\n",
